10. Processes & scheduling  hub

What's running, who started it, what it costs, and how to stop it.

On your machine
  Total     8 processes: 1 running, 3 sleeping, 1 waiting on I/O (D), 1 idle
            kernel thread, 2 zombies
  Zombies   PID 4242 worker (old), parent 4100 supervisor
            PID 4300 orphan-job, parent 9999
  Waiting   PID 5000 rsync, parent 1 systemd

  Memory    supervisor (793.0 MiB, PID 4100), rsync (239.3 MiB, PID 5000),
            systemd (11.7 MiB, PID 1)
  CPU time  rsync (1 min 35 s, PID 5000), supervisor (25 s, PID 4100), systemd
            (5 s, PID 1), kthreadd (0 s, PID 2), kworker/0:0-events (0 s, PID 3)

Commands
  ps aux --sort=-%mem | head  processes using the most memory
  top                         live view; press M for memory, P for CPU
  pgrep -a nginx              find processes by name, with their command
  pstree -p                   who started whom, with PIDs
  kill 1234                   ask a process to stop
  systemd-cgls                processes grouped by service and session

Paths
  /proc/PID/        everything about one process
  /proc/PID/status  state, memory, threads, owner

Cards
  idk 10.1  Process states and zombies
  idk 10.2  Signals
  idk 10.3  nice and priorities
  idk 10.4  cgroups
  idk 1.5   How a program starts: fork and exec
  idk 1.2   How the kernel shares the CPUs
  idk 12.1  Load average
  idk 11.2  The OOM killer
